{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The B&O's Capitol Limited, its Chicago-Washington flagship, inaugurated May 12 1923 as the road's entry into the New York-Chicago market against the Pennsylvania and the New York Central. GEOMETRY REBUILT (2026-08-24): the previous version borrowed the modern Amtrak alignment for this corridor on the assumption that the historical train ran the same physical line. That holds east of Pittsburgh but NOT west of it: Amtrak's Capitol Limited runs the ex-New York Central Water Level Route to Cleveland and then the ex-P&LE to Pittsburgh, while the B&O's own train ran its own railroad the whole way -- south of that, through Garrett, Defiance, Deshler, Fostoria, Tiffin, Willard, Akron and Youngstown. The old geometry therefore drew the wrong railroad across Indiana and Ohio, including a single 110 km straight hop across the north-west corner of Ohio. The route is now traced station by station from the June 1941 Capitol Limited timetable: Chicago Grand Central - South Chicago - Gary - La Paz - Garrett - Fostoria - Tiffin - Willard - Akron Union Station - Youngstown - Pittsburgh (P&LE Station) - McKeesport - Connellsville - Cumberland - Martinsburg - Silver Spring - Washington Union Station. Era boundaries follow real, sourced milestones; headway_min and avg_speed_kmh within each era are estimates, not from real timetables.", "sources": ["https://www.streamlinerschedules.com/concourse/track3/capitolltd194106.html (Capitol Limited, June 1941 timetable). Stations and mileposts, measured from New York, used both as the route and as the check on it: Chicago Grand Central 997.2, Chicago Sixty-third Street 987.7, South Chicago 978.0, Gary 963.9, La Paz 908.7, Garrett 846.9, Fostoria 755.7, Tiffin 743.2, Willard 719.0, Akron Union Station 644.5, Youngstown 591.3, Pittsburgh P.&L.E. Station 525.8, McKeesport 511.0, Connellsville 467.9, Cumberland 375.7, Martinsburg 298.0, Silver Spring 231.5, Washington Union Station 224.6, and on east to Baltimore Camden 187.8, Philadelphia Chestnut Street 91.9 and the Jersey City terminal and New York Liberty Street ferry at 1.0 and 0.0. Chicago to Washington is therefore 772.6 miles.", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capitol_Limited_(B%26O_train) (inaugurated May 12 1923, New York to Chicago via Washington, Baltimore and Pittsburgh; the B&O gave up competing with the Pennsylvania Railroad into New York and discontinued all passenger service north of Baltimore Apr 26 1958; discontinued Apr 30/May 1 1971 with the Amtrak takeover; 18 hours 40 minutes Baltimore-Chicago in 1956).", "https://www.american-rails.com/capitol.html and borhs.org (general history, retained from the earlier version of this layer).", "Deshler, Fostoria, Willard, Garrett, Akron Union Station and Youngstown are independently confirmed as Capitol Limited stops by railfanguides.us, Wikipedia's Fostoria and Garrett station articles, and the Willard and Garrett Subdivision articles."], "not_yet_sourced": "Frequencies and speeds in every era are estimates, not from timetables -- only the station mileposts are sourced. The pre-1958 New York section beyond Washington (Baltimore, Philadelphia, Jersey City and the Liberty Street ferry, all in the 1941 timetable above) is not drawn here; this layer covers Chicago to Washington only. The Chicago Sixty-third Street stop is likewise not drawn as a separate point.", "geometry_sources": ["OpenStreetMap via Overpass API, shortest-path search over the OSM rail graph between the 1941 timetable stations, run twice (plain rail, and with a wide gap stitch) and merged.", "Legs were validated by SPAN rather than individually: the legs between each pair of timetable stations are summed and compared with the booked mileage difference, accepted at 0.85-1.25x. This mattered twice. Judging legs individually against crow-flight distance accepted a 31% detour between Garrett and Fostoria, and rejected the Connellsville-Cumberland climb over Sand Patch as 'too long' when its curvature is exactly what the booked mileage says it should be. The span check reversed both.", "Result: 12 of 16 timetable spans traced on real track, giving 1248 km against the booked 772.6 miles (1243 km), a 1.00x match (the previous geometry was 316 points; this is 727). Spans still drawn as straight lines, with the booked distance each should cover: XX Chicago -> South Chicago: 18.9km (0.61x) vs 30.9km booked -- STRAIGHT LINE used | XX Garrett -> Fostoria: 191.7km (1.31x) vs 146.8km booked -- STRAIGHT LINE used | XX Fostoria -> Tiffin: 26.2km (1.30x) vs 20.1km booked -- STRAIGHT LINE used | XX Silver Spring -> Union Station: 15.8km (1.43x) vs 11.1km booked -- STRAIGHT LINE used", "The Garrett-Fostoria span is the notable straight-line case: routing wandered 31% long with wide search boxes and 47% long with a tight corridor, so it is drawn as a straight chain through the correct intermediate towns of Defiance and Deshler. Those towns are very nearly collinear, so the chain comes to about 146 km against 146.8 booked -- the length is right and only the local shape is approximate.", "Per-span tracing result: XX Chicago -> South Chicago: 18.9km (0.61x) vs 30.9km booked -- STRAIGHT LINE used | ok South Chicago -> Gary: 27.4km vs 22.7km booked (1.21x) [rail] | ok Gary -> La Paz: 90.7km vs 88.8km booked (1.02x) [rail] | ok La Paz -> Garrett: 99.8km vs 99.5km booked (1.00x) [rail] | XX Garrett -> Fostoria: 191.7km (1.31x) vs 146.8km booked -- STRAIGHT LINE used | XX Fostoria -> Tiffin: 26.2km (1.30x) vs 20.1km booked -- STRAIGHT LINE used | ok Tiffin -> Willard: 39.4km vs 38.9km booked (1.01x) [rail] | ok Willard -> Akron: 115.1km vs 119.9km booked (0.96x) [rail] | ok Akron -> Youngstown: 86.8km vs 85.6km booked (1.01x) [stitched] | ok Youngstown -> Pittsburgh: 116.6km vs 105.4km booked (1.11x) [stitched] via New Castle | ok Pittsburgh -> McKeesport: 28.9km vs 23.8km booked (1.21x) [stitched] | ok McKeesport -> Connellsville: 70.2km vs 69.4km booked (1.01x) [rail] | ok Connellsville -> Cumberland: 153.6km vs 148.4km booked (1.03x) [rail] via Rockwood, Meyersdale | ok Cumberland -> Martinsburg: 122.0km vs 125.0km booked (0.98x) [rail] via Hancock | ok Martinsburg -> Silver Spring: 110.0km vs 107.0km booked (1.03x) [rail] via Harpers Ferry, Brunswick, Point of Rocks, Rockville | XX Silver Spring -> Union Station: 15.8km (1.43x) vs 11.1km booked -- STRAIGHT LINE used"], "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6.", "pulled": "20260824"}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Baltimore and Ohio Railroad", "line": "Baltimore and Ohio: Capitol Limited", "id": "bo-capitol-limited", "color": "#00447C", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 72, "speed_source": "estimated/blended across the line's history; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1923, "end": 1958, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The Capitol Limited entered service May 12 1923 as the B&O's entry into the fiercely competitive New York-Chicago market, pitched squarely against New York Central's 20th Century Limited and the Pennsylvania's Broadway Limited. It originally ran all the way to New York via Washington and Baltimore, and was the B&O flagship for nearly 48 years, known for its personalized service and dining cars. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Capitol Limited - B&O train), american-rails.com, borhs.org"]}, {"start": 1958, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "B&O gave up competing with the Pennsylvania Railroad for New York traffic and dropped all passenger service north of Baltimore on Apr 26 1958; from then the Capitol Limited ran Washington-Chicago. Amtrak took over May 1 1971 and later revived the name; today this corridor is the northern leg of Amtrak's Floridian, which spliced the Capitol Limited and Silver Star routes in Nov 2024 - see amtrak.geojson. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Capitol Limited - B&O train), american-rails.com, borhs.org"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-87.62442, 41.87556], "to": [-87.55132, 41.73459], "chord_mi": 10.48, "traced_mi": 11.73, "snap_m": [117, 275], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["University Park Sub District (7.46 mi)", "South Chicago Sub District (4.23 mi)", "Metra (0.04 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-86.96077, 41.51732], "to": [-86.48411, 41.4696], "chord_mi": 25.36, "traced_mi": 24.9, "snap_m": [0, 1], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["CSX Garrett Subdivision (24.9 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-86.14062, 41.4513], "to": [-85.66234, 41.41441], "chord_mi": 25.36, "traced_mi": 24.91, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["CSX Garrett Subdivision (24.91 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-83.89911, 41.20755], "to": [-83.41411, 41.15743], "chord_mi": 25.82, "traced_mi": 28.6, "snap_m": [134, 257], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["CSX Willard Subdivision (25.17 mi)", "Norfolk and Western Railway [razed] (1.83 mi)", "(rail) (1.14 mi)", "NS Fostoria District (0.32 mi)", "CSX Toledo Subd